# Git's `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default, so without this a fresh
# Windows clone checks every text file out with CRLF. Two things break on that
# tree with no per-machine configuration to blame: the SKILL.md frontmatter
# parser, which matched `---\n` only, and `prettier --check`, whose
# `endOfLine: lf` default then reports every file. (#331)
#
# `text=auto` keeps Git's own binary detection; `eol=lf` applies to the files
# it decides are text, in the working tree as well as the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary by declaration rather than by detection: a heuristic that guesses
# wrong here corrupts the file rather than merely reformatting it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.wasm binary
*.sqlite binary
*.db binary
